From dfc746958f6df40963a1d44c80172213d69f7c19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:50:22 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox, agent_ui, agent_servers: the jail's messages
 and dialogs speak pt-BR

Closes the Agent Sandbox screen begun in 0178: what the jail says outside the
settings page now reads through zeo_i18n's t! from
locales/<language>/agent-jail.ftl, in en-US and pt-BR --

- why a start was refused (LaunchCause, LaunchError) and why a writable folder
  or an allowed host was refused (RwMapError, HostError, LimitError, the scan's
  WalkStop), the ai-memory errors and the outdated ai-jail warning;
- the Turn Off, Forget and Allow (secret-carrying MCP server) dialogs and the
  settings-write notification;
- the thread's lock: its tooltip lines, the pending and drifted lines, the
  reserved and shared CPUs, the Pause/Stop buttons and the paused controls,
  and the quick modal;
- the MCP not-enabled notice in the thread, with its title's plural.

The en-US file is the code's English byte for byte, so every existing test pins
the same text. A rule that refuses a path used to be a fragment glued into two
sentences ("Writable folder ... {rule}.", "The project folder ... {rule}; ...");
it is now a $rule select in each message (validate::rule_args), so a language
writes each sentence whole. The host shapes select on $shape the same way.

These errors are Display texts, and that is deliberate: a refused start reaches
the thread's error banner -- Zed's code -- as that text, so translating Display
is the only way to translate it without patching Zed's UI. The consequence: a
log line that quotes one of these errors follows the UI language. Its event=
and key= fields stay English, so the line stays machine-readable.

Constants that held text became functions (fail_closed, memory_port_unsupported,
turn_off_buttons, forget_buttons, secret_buttons, drifted_line, cpu_shared_label,
ai_jail_missing, paused_label, operator_paused_label) and their tests follow.
The prompt buttons are built per call; the answer is still read by index.

In the ai-jail group, after 0178: every file it edits belongs to that group.
